    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "15 Criminal offences relating to breaching a condition of a consent",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-15"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.86",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The consent of the Secretary under section 14 or 14A may be\ngiven:\n(a) unconditionally or subject to conditions; or\n(b) in respect of particular goods or classes of goods.\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(5) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent.\nPenalty: 500 penalty units.\n(6) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent.\nPenalty: 100 penalty units.\n(7) An offence against subsection (6) is an offence of strict liability.\n\nMedicines and other therapeutic goods that are not medical devices  Chapter 3\nStandards  Part 3-1\n\nSection 15AA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The consent of the Secretary under section 14 or 14A may be\ngiven:\n(a) unconditionally or subject to conditions; or\n(b) in respect of particular goods or classes of goods.\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(5) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent.\nPenalty: 500 penalty units.\n(6) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent.\nPenalty: 100 penalty units.\n(7) An offence against subsection (6) is an offence of strict liability.\n\nMedicines and other therapeutic goods that are not medical devices  Chapter 3\nStandards  Part 3-1\n\nSection 15AA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-6"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-7"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "24f355e6f2a95a6e0ef2666b16ff2094c60f3edcdcefcdec04900907f68a8cf1"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-15",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "15 Criminal offences relating to breaching a condition of a consent",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 86,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The consent of the Secretary under section 14 or 14A may be\ngiven:\n(a) unconditionally or subject to conditions; or\n(b) in respect of particular goods or classes of goods.\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(5) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent.\nPenalty: 500 penalty units.\n(6) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent.\nPenalty: 100 penalty units.\n(7) An offence against subsection (6) is an offence of strict liability.\n\nMedicines and other therapeutic goods that are not medical devices  Chapter 3\nStandards  Part 3-1\n\nSection 15AA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025"
    },
    {
