    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-2",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "15(2) Criminal offences relating to breaching a condition of a consent",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-15-ss-2"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.86",
      "values:originalText": "(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.",
      "skos:definition": "(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-2-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-2-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-2-logic-deontic-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-2-logic-alleninterval-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-2-logic-argumentation-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-15-ss-2-logic-diffusion-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "edf552051b0fdf7d71aa89b27a6ac030d5ee1c44f9c450b3ba8a6bc5a94a384e",
      "skos:note": "A person commits an offence if they breach a condition of consent."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-15-ss-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "15(2) Criminal offences relating to breaching a condition of a consent",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 86,
      "values:originalText": "(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA."
    },
    {
