    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-26bf",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "26BF Permissible indications",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-26bf"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.186",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Minister may, by legislative instrument, make a determination\nin relation to either or both of the following:\n(a) indications;\n(b) requirements in relation to indications.\nNote:\nSee paragraphs 26A(2)(fba), (fd) and (fe) (which deal with matters\nthat a person seeking the listing of a medicine under section 26A must\ncertify).\n(2) In deciding whether to make a determination under subsection (1)\nin relation to a particular indication, the Minister may have regard\nto whether the indication is a therapeutic use that relates to one or\nmore of the following:\n(a) maintaining health;\n(b) enhancing health;\n(c) preventing a dietary deficiency;\n\nMedicines and other therapeutic goods that are not medical devices  Chapter 3\nRegistration and listing of therapeutic goods  Part 3-2\nRegistration and listing  Division 2\nSection 26BG\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(d) a disease, ailment, defect or injury, other than a serious form\nof the disease, ailment, defect or injury.\n(3) Subsection (2) does not limit the matters to which the Minister may\nhave regard in deciding whether to make a determination under\nsubsection (1) in relation to a particular indication.\n(4) Without limiting paragraph (1)(b), the requirements may relate to:\n(a) the use of particular indications in specified circumstances;\nor\n(b) the use of particular indications if certain specified conditions\nare met.\n(5) A determination under paragraph (1)(b) may make different\nprovision for different classes of medicines.\n(6) Despite subsection 14(2) of the Legislation Act 2003, a\ndetermination under subsection (1) of this section may make\nprovision in relation to a matter by applying, adopting or\nincorporating, with or without modification, any matter contained\nin an instrument or other writing as in force or existing from time\nto time.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Minister may, by legislative instrument, make a determination\nin relation to either or both of the following:\n(a) indications;\n(b) requirements in relation to indications.\nNote:\nSee paragraphs 26A(2)(fba), (fd) and (fe) (which deal with matters\nthat a person seeking the listing of a medicine under section 26A must\ncertify).\n(2) In deciding whether to make a determination under subsection (1)\nin relation to a particular indication, the Minister may have regard\nto whether the indication is a therapeutic use that relates to one or\nmore of the following:\n(a) maintaining health;\n(b) enhancing health;\n(c) preventing a dietary deficiency;\n\nMedicines and other therapeutic goods that are not medical devices  Chapter 3\nRegistration and listing of therapeutic goods  Part 3-2\nRegistration and listing  Division 2\nSection 26BG\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(d) a disease, ailment, defect or injury, other than a serious form\nof the disease, ailment, defect or injury.\n(3) Subsection (2) does not limit the matters to which the Minister may\nhave regard in deciding whether to make a determination under\nsubsection (1) in relation to a particular indication.\n(4) Without limiting paragraph (1)(b), the requirements may relate to:\n(a) the use of particular indications in specified circumstances;\nor\n(b) the use of particular indications if certain specified conditions\nare met.\n(5) A determination under paragraph (1)(b) may make different\nprovision for different classes of medicines.\n(6) Despite subsection 14(2) of the Legislation Act 2003, a\ndetermination under subsection (1) of this section may make\nprovision in relation to a matter by applying, adopting or\nincorporating, with or without modification, any matter contained\nin an instrument or other writing as in force or existing from time\nto time.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-26bf-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-26bf-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-26bf-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-26bf-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-26bf-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-26bf-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "c29e1efc49a0f59edffe8e0093141ff501d6dfc7e7f47f62a53765e84f8b4984"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-26bf",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "26BF Permissible indications",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 186,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Minister may, by legislative instrument, make a determination\nin relation to either or both of the following:\n(a) indications;\n(b) requirements in relation to indications.\nNote:\nSee paragraphs 26A(2)(fba), (fd) and (fe) (which deal with matters\nthat a person seeking the listing of a medicine under section 26A must\ncertify).\n(2) In deciding whether to make a determination under subsection (1)\nin relation to a particular indication, the Minister may have regard\nto whether the indication is a therapeutic use that relates to one or\nmore of the following:\n(a) maintaining health;\n(b) enhancing health;\n(c) preventing a dietary deficiency;\n\nMedicines and other therapeutic goods that are not medical devices  Chapter 3\nRegistration and listing of therapeutic goods  Part 3-2\nRegistration and listing  Division 2\nSection 26BG\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(d) a disease, ailment, defect or injury, other than a serious form\nof the disease, ailment, defect or injury.\n(3) Subsection (2) does not limit the matters to which the Minister may\nhave regard in deciding whether to make a determination under\nsubsection (1) in relation to a particular indication.\n(4) Without limiting paragraph (1)(b), the requirements may relate to:\n(a) the use of particular indications in specified circumstances;\nor\n(b) the use of particular indications if certain specified conditions\nare met.\n(5) A determination under paragraph (1)(b) may make different\nprovision for different classes of medicines.\n(6) Despite subsection 14(2) of the Legislation Act 2003, a\ndetermination under subsection (1) of this section may make\nprovision in relation to a matter by applying, adopting or\nincorporating, with or without modification, any matter contained\nin an instrument or other writing as in force or existing from time\nto time."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-26bf-ss-1",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "26BF(1) Permissible indications",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-26bf-ss-1"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
